A||||10||burdell1||Slow Power Window
Z||000000||burdell1||04-27-99||10:24 PM||||I am having a problem with my driver's side power window. It is really slow, and when both windows are rolled up simultaneously, it will stop. Does it just need to be lubricated (tried that, didn't seem to help) or do I need a new motor?  
Z||000001||Carrolles||04-28-99||06:48 AM||||It sounds like you need to clean the armature and brushes and possibly replace the brushes in the motor.  Use some very fine empry paper or an eraser to clean the armature.<p>The lift mechanism is riveted in and is a bit of a pain to remove and replace.  Here is a link to a good procedure for installing power windows that I got off of one of the Fiero Club message boards.  Maybe you can use the procedure in your repair.<p>http://www.tir.com/~jaski/pw_pl_install.htm<p>Good luck,<br>Carroll
Z||000002||Carrolles||04-28-99||03:03 PM||||It sounds like you need to clean the commutator and brushes.  Use a fine emory paper or an eraser to clean the commutator.<br>The lift mechanism is riveted in and is difficult to get to.  Here is the URL for a procedure that I found on one of the Fiero message boards that might help:<p>http://www.tir.com/~jaski/pw_pl_install.htm<p>You might want to start with the cheap easy stuff first like checking that all of the wiring connectors are tight and the contacts are good.  I believe the wiring connector for the lift motor is on the back side of the motor and is rather difficult to get to.<p>I have manual windows and locks and hope to upgrade soon.<br>Good luck,<br>Carroll
Z||000003||Carrolles||04-28-99||03:04 PM||||Oops.<br>Sorry for the repeat<br>Carroll<br>
Z||000004||ezsurf||04-28-99||08:36 PM||||Carrolles,<br>I have an 86 SE with manual windows.  I'm working on the engine right now, but I had the idea of putting power windows and power door locks in later.  I don't know if the wiring harness is already there for the windows and door locks, but I suspect it is.  You mentioned upgrading your windows.  How difficult is this to do? 
Z||000005||Carrolles||04-29-99||06:46 AM||||The Cross Frame Wiring Harness which powers the windows and locks is only installed in vehicles equipped with these options.  It runs under the dash and requires removal of the dash to install in its original position.  I believe it can be installed without removing the dash but it might be a bit of a pain to do so.  I'm getting too old for laying upside down with my head under the steering column for any length of time.<p>Ed Parks (Fiero Factory) says the easiest way to upgrade is to buy a set of doors that have the options and get the harness and switches out from the donor car.  All Fiero doors interchange and since they were all assembled on the same Mill and Drill Jig they should line up pretty close.  Swap the door panel and outer skin between the two sets of doors.<br>I'm looking at an 85GT that is pretty rough but has what I need.  I may buy the car, swap parts, clean up the car and resell it.<br>Ed could probably supply the correct door panels if you need them. He can be reached at    Fierofactory@juno.com or (256) 420-5391.<p>The URL I listed in the previous post has a procedure for adding power options to your existing doors.<br>I looked into the JC Whitney set and found that when installed they use the original window crank shaft and have a plastic cap covering the hole in the door panel and the protruding shaft.  I'd rather stay as original as possible with my car.<p>Good luck,<br>Carroll
Z||000006||Carrolles||04-29-99||06:53 AM||||Ezsurf,<br>I forgot to mention, I have a Helms Manual for my 85 and the 22P Parts Illustration manual (covers all years).  If you need I can send you a copy of the wiring harness layout and wiring diagram when I get home this weekend.<p>By the way, Ed Parks is hosting a Fiero Swap Meet at the Fiero Factory in the Huntsville Alabama area tomorrow and Saturday and expects 200 to 300 attendees.  I'm going and hope to find what I need .  I'm also thinking of adding cruise control to my car.<br>Thanks<br>Carroll
Z||000007||burdell1||04-30-99||08:44 AM||||Where are the brushes and armature?  Where would one get new replacement brushes?
Z||000008||Carrolles||05-04-99||12:05 PM||||The armature is on the motor shaft inside the motor housing.  It will be made of copper.  The brushes contact the armature.  You should be able to get brushes from Pontiac or any good electrical supply house.  Take the motor to match up to new brushes.
Z||000009||burdell1||05-26-99||12:46 PM||||Would it be easier to just replace the windshield motor?<br>
Z||000010||Carrolles||05-26-99||04:33 PM||||Replacing the motor would be easier than rebuilding it since the hard part of the job is getting the motor in and out of the car.
